To mitigate the effects of resource reductions, the Mission has adopted the following measures:
	(a)	Strengthened regional hub model for field offices. The Mazar-e Sharif field office functions as a northern hub, with an expanded area of responsibility from four to six provinces, including Baghlan and Faryab. It is supported by redeployed posts from the two closed field offices and operational resources to sustain regular travel and engagement deemed essential for the continued coverage of the two provinces; 
	(b)	Continuity of local engagement. Two staff members from each of the closing field offices were selected to fill newly established positions in the Mazar-e Sharif field office, preserving institutional knowledge, access and transition;

	(c)	Alternative modalities. Increased use of virtual engagement complements reduced in-person presence, including for interaction with women and other key interlocutors;
	(d)	Partnership arrangements. Collaboration and cost-sharing with United Nations partners will continue to support the implementation of mandate-related activities in cross-cutting or complementary areas of engagement; 
With regard to the climate, peace and security capacity transition, the extrabudgetary-funded Climate, Peace and Security Adviser, funded until the end of 2027, will be supported by redeployed staff, enabling the practical transfer of knowledge and deeper familiarization with the function. Following the cessation of extrabudgetary funding, relevant tasks will be absorbed using existing resources and capabilities, reinforced by the technical expertise and related programmatic activities of United Nations partners.
